---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Lean Body Mass Calculator (LBM) — Calculate Lean Body Mass"
  description="Calculate lean body mass (LBM) using pounds and inches or kilograms and centimeters. Estimate lean mass from body fat percentage or with the Boer, James, or Hume formula."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    "name": "Lean Body Mass Calculator",
    "applicationCategory": "HealthApplication",
    "operatingSystem": "Any",
    "offers": {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "price": "0",
      "priceCurrency": "USD"
    },
    "description": "Free calculator for estimating lean body mass from body fat percentage or using the Boer, James, and Hume predictive equations."
  })} />

  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Lean Body Mass Calculator (LBM)</h1>

    <p>
      Calculate your lean body mass using pounds and inches or kilograms and
      centimeters. Use your body-fat percentage for a direct estimate, or
      estimate LBM with the Boer, James, or Hume formula.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="calc-card" aria-labelledby="calculator-heading">
    <h2 id="calculator-heading">Calculate Your Lean Body Mass</h2>

    <div class="field">
      <label>Unit</label>

      <div
        class="unit-toggle"
        role="group"
        aria-label="Choose measurement unit"
      >
        <button
          type="button"
          id="unit-lb"
          class="active"
          aria-pressed="true"
        >
          lb
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="unit-kg"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          kg
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label>Calculation Method</label>

      <div
        class="unit-toggle method-toggle"
        role="group"
        aria-label="Choose lean body mass calculation method"
      >
        <button
          type="button"
          id="method-bodyfat"
          class="active"
          aria-pressed="true"
        >
          Body Fat %
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="method-boer"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          Boer
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="method-james"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          James
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="method-hume"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          Hume
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="field" id="sex-field" hidden>
      <label for="sex">Sex</label>

      <select id="sex">
        <option value="male">Male</option>
        <option value="female">Female</option>
      </select>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label for="weight">Body Weight</label>

      <input
        type="number"
        id="weight"
        placeholder="e.g. 180"
        inputmode="decimal"
        min="25"
        max="660"
        step="0.1"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="field" id="bodyfat-field">
      <label for="bodyfat">Body Fat Percentage</label>

      <div class="input-with-suffix">
        <input
          type="number"
          id="bodyfat"
          placeholder="e.g. 20"
          inputmode="decimal"
          min="1"
          max="69.9"
          step="0.1"
        />
        <span>%</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="height-fields" hidden>
      <div class="field" id="height-imperial">
        <label>Height</label>

        <div class="height-grid">
          <div>
            <label for="height-ft" class="sr-only">Feet</label>

            <input
              type="number"
              id="height-ft"
              placeholder="ft"
              inputmode="numeric"
              min="3"
              max="8"
              step="1"
            />
          </div>

          <div>
            <label for="height-in" class="sr-only">Inches</label>

            <input
              type="number"
              id="height-in"
              placeholder="in"
              inputmode="decimal"
              min="0"
              max="11.99"
              step="0.1"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="field" id="height-metric" hidden>
        <label for="height-cm">Height</label>

        <div class="input-with-suffix">
          <input
            type="number"
            id="height-cm"
            placeholder="e.g. 175"
            inputmode="decimal"
            min="120"
            max="250"
            step="0.1"
          />
          <span>cm</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div
      id="input-error"
      class="input-error"
      role="alert"
      aria-live="assertive"
      hidden
    ></div>

    <button class="btn-primary" id="calc-btn">
      Calculate Lean Body Mass
    </button>

    <div
      class="result-box"
      id="result-box"
      aria-live="polite"
      hidden
    >
      <div class="big-number" id="result-lbm">–</div>

      <div class="label" id="result-label">
        Estimated Lean Body Mass
      </div>

      <div class="composition-visual" aria-label="Body composition estimate">
        <div class="composition-bar">
          <div
            class="composition-lean"
            id="composition-lean"
          ></div>

          <div
            class="composition-fat"
            id="composition-fat"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <div class="composition-legend">
          <span>
            <i class="legend-dot lean-dot"></i>
            Lean Mass
            <strong id="visual-lean-percent">–</strong>
          </span>

          <span>
            <i class="legend-dot fat-dot"></i>
            Fat Mass
            <strong id="visual-fat-percent">–</strong>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="lbm-details">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Body Weight</span>
          <strong id="result-weight">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Estimated Fat Mass</span>
          <strong id="result-fat">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Lean Mass Percentage</span>
          <strong id="result-percent">–</strong>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="range-result" id="method-result">
        <strong>Method:</strong>
        <span id="result-method">–</span>
      </div>

      <div class="lifter-note">
        <strong>Important:</strong>
        Lean body mass is not the same thing as skeletal muscle mass.
        It includes non-fat tissues such as body water, organs, bones,
        skin, connective tissue, and muscle.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>What Exactly Is Lean Body Mass?</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Lean body mass (LBM)</strong> is commonly used to describe
      the part of body weight that is not body fat. Depending on the
      measurement method and terminology used, related terms such as
      <strong>fat-free mass</strong> and <strong>lean mass</strong> may be
      defined differently.
    </p>

    <p>
      For this calculator, the practical calculation is:
      <strong>lean body mass = total body weight − estimated fat mass.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, if someone weighs 180 lb and has an estimated 20% body
      fat, their estimated fat mass is 36 lb and their estimated lean body
      mass is about 144 lb.
    </p>

    <p>
      LBM should not be interpreted as a measurement of skeletal muscle
      alone. Muscle is only one component of the non-fat portion of body
      weight.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>The Math Behind Lean Body Mass Estimates</h2>

    <p>
      There are two main approaches available in this calculator. If you
      already have a body-fat estimate, lean mass can be calculated directly
      from body weight and estimated fat mass. If body-fat percentage is not
      available, predictive equations can estimate lean body mass from
      measurements such as height, weight, and sex.
    </p>

    <h3>Using Body Fat Percentage</h3>

    <p>
      When an estimated body-fat percentage is available:
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>Fat Mass = Body Weight × Body Fat %</code><br />
      <code>Lean Body Mass = Body Weight − Fat Mass</code>
    </p>

    <p>
      This method is only as accurate as the body-fat estimate used as its
      input. Different methods, including DEXA, skinfolds, tape-measure
      equations, and consumer BIA devices, can produce different estimates.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you do not know your body-fat percentage, you can first use our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator">
        Body Fat Calculator
      </a>
      to obtain a tape-measure-based estimate.
    </p>

    <h3>Boer Formula</h3>

    <p>
      For men:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 0.407 × weight(kg) + 0.267 × height(cm) − 19.2
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      For women:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 0.252 × weight(kg) + 0.473 × height(cm) − 48.3
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      The Boer equation estimates lean body mass from body weight and height.
      The original work examined estimated lean body mass as an index for
      normalizing body-fluid volumes. 3
    </p>

    <h3>James Formula</h3>

    <p>
      The James equation uses body weight in kilograms and height in meters.
    </p>

    <p>
      For men:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 1.10 × weight(kg) − 128 × (weight(kg) / height(m))²
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      For women:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 1.07 × weight(kg) − 148 × (weight(kg) / height(m))²
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      The unit conversion is important: using height in centimeters directly
      in the James equation produces an incorrect result. Published versions
      of the equation specify height in meters. 4
    </p>

    <h3>Hume Formula</h3>

    <p>
      For men:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 0.32810 × weight(kg) + 0.33929 × height(cm) − 29.5336
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      For women:
      <br />
      <code>
        LBM = 0.29569 × weight(kg) + 0.41813 × height(cm) − 43.2933
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      Hume's original study developed sex-specific equations for estimating
      lean body mass from height and weight. 5
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Lean Body Mass, Fat-Free Mass, and Muscle Mass</h2>

    <p>
      These terms are related but should not automatically be treated as
      identical measurements.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Muscle mass:</strong> refers to muscle tissue rather than
        every non-fat component of the body.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Fat-free mass:</strong> generally refers to body mass other
        than body fat.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Lean mass / LBM:</strong> terminology varies by measurement
        method, calculator, study, and body-composition model.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Modern body-composition standards recommend using precise terminology
      because fat-free mass, lean mass, lean soft tissue, and skeletal muscle
      represent different concepts. 6
    </p>

    <p>
      RepMaths uses the familiar term <strong>Lean Body Mass (LBM)</strong>
      because it is widely used in fitness searches and calculator queries,
      while explaining these terminology limitations rather than presenting
      LBM as a direct measurement of muscle.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Applying LBM to Your Nutrition Strategy</h2>

    <p>
      Body weight alone does not show how much of that weight comes from fat
      versus non-fat tissues. LBM provides additional body-composition
      context and can be useful when tracking changes over time.
    </p>

    <p>
      LBM is also used in some metabolic equations. For example, the
      Katch-McArdle equation estimates resting energy expenditure using
      lean body mass. When LBM is estimated rather than directly measured,
      the resulting calorie estimate is also an estimate.
    </p>

    <p>
      For practical calorie planning, compare your result with our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/bmr-calculator">BMR Calculator</a>
      and
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator">TDEE Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      LBM can also provide context when setting protein targets. However,
      protein recommendations can be based on total body weight and training
      goal as well. Use our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/protein-calculator">
        Protein Intake Calculator
      </a>
      for a goal-based protein estimate.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Lean Mass Percentage</h2>

    <p>
      Lean mass percentage represents the estimated portion of your body
      weight that is lean rather than body fat:
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>
        Lean Mass % = (Lean Body Mass ÷ Body Weight) × 100
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, someone with an estimated 20% body fat has approximately
      80% of their body weight classified as lean by this simple calculation.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no single universal "ideal" lean-mass percentage for every
      adult. Interpretation varies with sex, age, training status, body
      composition, and the method used to estimate body fat.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>What About FFMI?</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Fat-Free Mass Index (FFMI)</strong> adjusts fat-free mass for
      height, making it useful for comparing lean mass relative to body size.
      FFMI is related to LBM but is a separate calculation.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want to compare your estimated lean mass with your height, use
      our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/ffmi-calculator">
        FFMI Calculator
      </a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>How Accurate Is a Lean Body Mass Calculator?</h2>

    <p>
      A calculator does not directly measure your body's tissue composition.
      Its result depends on the calculation method and the quality of the
      input data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Research comparing height-and-weight equations with direct imaging
      methods shows that predictive equations can correlate with measured
      lean mass while still producing meaningful individual differences.
      In one study comparing Hume, Boer, and James estimates with CT-derived
      measurements, none of the height-weight formulas was practically
      equivalent to direct CT measurement for individual estimates.
      7
    </p>

    <p>
      Hume's equation has also been compared with DXA, showing strong
      correlation but some systematic difference between estimated and
      measured lean body mass. 8
    </p>

    <p>
      For progress tracking, using the same measurement method consistently
      can be more useful than treating a single calculator result as an
      exact measurement.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Sources &amp; Methodology</h2>

    <p>
      RepMaths uses established anthropometric equations to provide
      practical estimates. These equations are not substitutes for direct
      body-composition measurement when precise measurement is required.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Hume, R.
        <em>Prediction of lean body mass from height and weight.</em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5929341/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Boer, P.
        <em>
          Estimated lean body mass as an index for normalization of body
          fluid volumes in humans.
        </em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6496691/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Carnevale V, Piscitelli PA, Minonne R, et al.
        <em>
          Estimate of body composition by Hume's equation: validation with
          DXA.
        </em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25209892/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Research comparing Hume, Boer, and James equations with
        CT-derived lean body mass:
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27688106/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      These sources support the use of predictive equations as estimates,
      while also demonstrating why calculator results should not be treated
      as direct measurements for every individual.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I lose body weight while increasing lean mass?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes. Body weight can decrease while estimated lean mass increases if
        fat mass decreases while lean tissue is maintained or gained. The
        amount of change depends on training, nutrition, starting body
        composition, and other individual factors.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does my smart scale show a different lean mass than this calculator?</h3>

      <p>
        The methods are different. Predictive equations estimate lean mass
        mainly from inputs such as height, weight, and sex, while consumer
        smart scales commonly use bioelectrical impedance. Both methods are
        estimates and can produce different results.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can creatine change my estimated lean body mass?</h3>

      <p>
        Creatine supplementation can increase body water, particularly
        intracellular water, and changes in body water can affect some body
        composition measurements and estimates. An increase in calculated
        lean mass should therefore not automatically be interpreted as an
        equivalent increase in newly built skeletal muscle.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How much of lean body mass is water?</h3>

      <p>
        Body water is a substantial component of fat-free and lean tissues,
        but a single percentage should not be treated as a precise rule for
        every person. Hydration status can also affect body-composition
        measurements and estimates.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which LBM method should I use?</h3>

      <p>
        If you have a reasonably accurate body-fat estimate, subtracting
        estimated fat mass from total body weight is a straightforward way
        to estimate lean mass. If body-fat percentage is unavailable, Boer,
        James, and Hume provide alternative predictive estimates.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use lean body mass to calculate my BMR?</h3>

      <p>
        Some metabolic equations, including Katch-McArdle, use lean body
        mass. Because the LBM itself may be estimated, the resulting BMR
        should also be treated as an estimate. You can compare it with our
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/bmr-calculator">
          BMR Calculator
        </a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is lean body mass the same as muscle mass?</h3>

      <p>
        No. Muscle is only one component of lean body mass. Lean mass also
        includes body water, organs, bones, skin, connective tissue, and
        other non-fat tissues.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is lean body mass the same as fat-free mass?</h3>

      <p>
        The terms are often used interchangeably in fitness contexts, but
        technical definitions can vary between body-composition models and
        measurement methods. For precise interpretation, use the definition
        provided by the particular method or study.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <RelatedTools currentToolId="lean-body-mass-calculator" />

  <style>
    .method-toggle {
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .height-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 10px;
    }

    .input-with-suffix {
      display: flex;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .input-with-suffix input {
      flex: 1;
      min-width: 0;
      border-radius: 8px 0 0 8px;
    }

    .input-with-suffix span {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      padding: 0 12px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-left: 0;
      border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
      background: var(--surface-2);
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .lbm-details {
